WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.567
Hi, everyone,
I’m Anu Srivastava,

00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:11.867
and I'm going to show you
how you can use Vertex AI

00:00:11.867 --> 00:00:16.000
to train and serve a TensorFlow model
using code in a custom container.

00:00:16.733 --> 00:00:20.267
We’ll be using a dataset
of technical specs of automobiles

00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:25.100
to build a model that can predict
the fuel efficiency of late 1970s

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:27.200
and early 1980s cars.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.933
You can find this dataset on Kaggle.

00:00:29.933 --> 00:00:35.033
We must provide the model with descriptions
of several automobiles from that time period.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.500
The description includes
attributes like cylinders,

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.900
displacement, horsepower, and weight.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.467
We're working mainly in a notebook.

00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:45.867
So, think of this as an interactive Web tool

00:00:45.867 --> 00:00:48.867
where you can combine
code, computational output,

00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:52.200
text, and multimedia resources
in a single home.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.500
We’ve set up a container here, a portable 
environment to work with our model.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.367
Don’t worry, we have all the source code
and instructions for you in a codelab

00:01:00.367 --> 00:01:01.700
to try this out at home.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:06.333
Now that everything's set up in here,
let's train the job on the platform.

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:11.100
I can go here to the Trainings tab,
create new job.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.400
I’ll name my demo,

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.200
add a few other settings
like our container URL and compute specs,

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.233
and now it's off to the races.

00:01:18.233 --> 00:01:20.767
This one actually takes
10 to 15 minutes to train.

00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:23.733
I ran a job ahead of time
so we can test it out together.

00:01:23.733 --> 00:01:26.633
So now that it’s trained,
we deploy an endpoint,

00:01:26.633 --> 00:01:29.000
and we can now use this
back in our notebook.

00:01:29.633 --> 00:01:31.833
Now for the moment of truth.

00:01:31.833 --> 00:01:33.600
Let's predict a sample.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.000
I’ve added a cell
down here with a sample case.

00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:40.267
This is a set of attributes
that we described earlier,

00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:41.767
but they've been normalized,

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:45.867
which is why they don’t look exactly like,
say, a year, or the weight of a car.

00:01:45.867 --> 00:01:49.367
I hit run, and we can see
that an automobile with these values

00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:53.533
would give us approximately
16 miles per gallon.

00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:57.633
To make this visual, I can use a lot 
of ready-to-go functionality

00:01:57.633 --> 00:01:59.433
to visualize my data down here.

00:01:59.433 --> 00:02:03.233
Use graphs and charts to share
your findings with your friends.

00:02:03.233 --> 00:02:06.800
We hope we’ve inspired you
to try working with your own data,

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:09.867
or to re-create this demo,
check out the links provided.

